# CODEOWNERS for OpenIDX
# https://docs.github.com/en/repositories/managing-your-repositorys-settings-and-features/customizing-your-repository/about-code-owners
#
# One line, on purpose.
#
# This file used to be 212 lines naming eighteen @openidx/* teams —
# maintainer, backend-team, identity-team, oauth-team, governance-team,
# devops-team and so on — none of which exist. The repository is
# mhmtgngr/openidx, a personal namespace: it cannot have organization teams at
# all. That is not a cosmetic problem. Under "Require review from Code Owners"
# GitHub cannot resolve an owner for any path, so either every rule silently
# no-ops (and the file describes a review process nobody performs) or every
# pull request is blocked with no reviewer who can clear it. The same phantom
# teams were already purged from renovate.json for the same reason.
#
# It also carried two `!` lines. GitHub's CODEOWNERS syntax has no negation —
# those patterns were read as literal paths beginning with "!", matched
# nothing, and made the file look like it exempted .github/workflows/ when it
# did not. And it assigned an owner to .github/dependabot.yml, deleted when the
# repository moved to Renovate.
#
# So: one owner, who is the actual maintainer, for everything. Per-area rules
# come back when there are per-area humans — a rule is only worth writing when
# it routes a review to someone who will do it. Ownership is enforced by this
# file plus the branch protection setting; the security-critical paths are
# marked in SECURITY.md and by the CI guards, not by fictional teams here.
#
# scripts/check-codeowners.sh keeps the mechanical defects out: no `!`
# negations, no rule for a path that does not exist, no rule without an owner.

